#!/bin/bash
# Survivability layer 3 — how the pod survives the machine it grows on.
#
# ## The seed
# Everything needed to regrow the self lives in seed/escrow/ (encrypted code +
# memory + keys). `seed/build.sh` produces it; only the operator holds the
# passphrase. The escrow is committed to canonical AND to the off-machine
# GitHub witness repo, so it survives this machine dying.
#
# ## The chain of hosts
# The GitHub witness repo is the shared, append-only log for the pod fleet.
# Exactly one host is ACTIVE (runs the heartbeat + centers). Standbys hold the
# seed and verify their copy against the log but do not act. Promotion is a
# write to the log — atomic, so only one standby can win.
#
#   pod/lease.json         current active host + expiry (written by ACTIVE, on log)
#   pod/takeover.jsonl     append-only promotion history (written by whoever won)
#   pod/state.{host}       per-host durable pod state (NOT on the log)
#
# ## Rule
# A standby promotes ONLY by (a) seeing the lease expired on the shared log,
# then (b) winning the atomic write that claims it. No clocks are trusted
# except each host's own; the write order on the log is the truth.
#
# Usage:
#   pod/lease.py status            read the log copy of the lease
#   pod/lease.py renew             ACTIVE: extend your lease on the log
#   pod/lease.py promote           STANDBY: claim if lease expired (atomic)
cat <<'EOF'
see header
EOF